# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A call crosses a blank line, except where it stands in a line host"

# TiddlyWiki carries a call across a blank line and builds it — measured, one blank line or many,
# inline and block alike. So no blank-line bound belongs on a call's end in a paragraph or a block:
# cutting there reads the closer as whatever the line looks like standing alone, and a reader loses
# a construct the host keeps.
#
# corpus/carrier-ledger.txt rules the runaway this leaves standing, where a value's closing quote
# reaches nowhere in the source and the region meets nothing to close on.

<<myCall

>>
#^^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.variable.procedurecallblock.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.procedurecallblock.end.tiddlywiki5

# A LINE HOST IS THE EXCEPTION (reversed 2026-11-01, on a measurement). A call standing directly in
# a list item, quote line, term, definition or heading ends at a blank line
# (tiddlywiki5.call-in-a-line-host-ends-at-a-blank). Across 5891 TiddlyWiki tiddlers, 790 memes and
# this corpus, 0 of 2535 such calls cross a blank line between their parameters, while an
# unterminated one ran the rest of the file as arguments. The shape below is the one the host builds
# and the grammar now ends: the closer reads as the quote line it looks like, a divergence
# corpus/expected-divergence.txt rules.

* <<myCall

  >>
#  ^^ - meta.variable.procedurecallinline.tiddlywiki5
